<pre class="link-defaults">
spec:html; type:dfn; text:allowed to use
</pre>

Note: This specification is under active development and therefore incomplete. If you're looking for an overview of the proposal, please refer to the [Explainer](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md).

Introduction {#intro}
=====================

*This section is non-normative.*

The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to give browsers the means to communicate directly, without the aid of a server in the middle. It is designed to enable this communication within the confines of a [=local communication medium=].

Many modern Web security measures rely on the presence of naming, signaling and certificate authorities. Local use-cases where these authorities are not readily available have started lagging behind in user experience or are not supported altogether. The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to bring back first-class support for local communication use-cases while working within the same strict user-friendliness, security and privacy requirements.

Examples of potential uses of this API include: Collaboration tools that work during an internet outage or emergency situations, connecting to your NAS, your home security system, your robotic assistant doing the dishes or your GPU farm in the basement that's running your personalized virtual assistant. See also [Use Cases](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md#use-cases).

This specification aims to strike a balance between creating a powerful new building block for developers and providing a seamless, secure and privacy preserving experience for browser users. As an example: while the API doesn't provide raw socket access, it does aim to give developers the flexibility to innovate on top by providing a persistent, two-way communication channel with little overhead.

The API is designed to be backed by an authenticated, streams-based transport. It is defined against the abstract operations of [[#transport-abstraction]], which a [=transport binding=] maps onto a concrete protocol. As a commitment to an open standards-based implementation path, this specification defines a binding to the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] in [[#osp-binding]]. Other bindings are anticipated, for example WebTransport over QUIC with peers authenticated on the local network as explored by the IETF [SETTLE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/settle/about/) working group.

Terminology {#terminology}
======================================================

The term <dfn>local communication medium</dfn> refers to a communication medium that provides a local, direct communication channel between the communicating devices. It carries IP between the devices, directly or through a local network, for example a wired or wireless local area network or Wi-Fi Direct. Discovery and authentication may also use other channels, such as Bluetooth Low Energy, NFC or a QR code. This spec will not mandate which underlying technologies must be supported.

The term <dfn>local communication</dfn> refers to data communication over the [=local communication medium=].

The term <dfn>communication medium topology</dfn> refers to the composition of the [=local communication medium=] which may include sensitive information such as IP or other address information or the number of devices connected to the medium.

Permission Policy Integration {#permission-policy-integration}
======================================================

The Local Peer-to-Peer API defines a [=policy-controlled feature=] identified by the token "local-peer-to-peer". Its [=default allowlist=] is `"self"`.

Workers (dedicated and shared) adhere to the permission policy set by their owning document(s):
<ul>
  <li>
    Dedicated workers can be created from other workers, in which case the permission policy of the first owning document (in case of a dedicated worker) or owning documents (in case of a shared worker) up the owner chain will be used.
  </li>
  <li>
    Shared workers often have multiple owning documents as they can be obtained by other documents with the [=same origin=]. In this case, all owning documents must be [=allowed to use=] the [=policy-controlled feature=] defined by this specification.
  </li>
</ul>

Note: There has been discussion on allowing setting permission policy directly on a worker on creation, in which case that would have to be consulted as well.
 
The [=policy-controlled feature/default allowlist=] of `"self"` allows usage in same-origin nested frames but prevents third-party content from using the feature. Third-party content usage can be selectively enabled by adding `allow="local-peer-to-peer"` attribute to the frame container element:

<pre class="example" title= "Enabling Local Peer-to-Peer API on third-party content" highlight='js'>
&lt;iframe src="https://third-party.com" allow="local-peer-to-peer"/&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
</pre>

Alternatively, the Local Peer-to-Peer API can be disabled completely by specifying the permissions policy in a HTTP response header:

<pre class="example" title="Permission Policy over HTTP" highlight='js'>
Permissions-Policy: local-peer-to-peer=()
</pre>

See [[!PERMISSIONS-POLICY]] for more details.

Peer Management {#peer-management}
==================================

Note: This section and its subsections use RFC 2119 terminology in a relaxed manner. These sections will be converted into well-defined algorithmic normative prose informed by further implementation experience. For now, this relaxed description better allows for rapid prototyping.

The user agent is in charge of managing peers.

A <dfn>peer</dfn> is an equal participant in the [=local communication=]. These nodes can communicate without the need for a central coordination by a server.

A user agent has an associated <dfn>local peer-to-peer manager</dfn> in charge of managing [=peers=]. Its responsibility is to [=start local peer discovery=], [=establish local peer connections=] and [=acquire a local peer grant=] on a per-origin basis. This is done to avoid exposing information about the [=communication medium topology=].

Local state {#known-peers}
--------------------------

The [=local peer-to-peer manager=] has:

: <dfn>local trust store</dfn>
:: A [=/set=] of [=trust anchors=], see [[#peer-authentication]].
: <dfn>known peers map</dfn>
:: An [=/ordered map=] from [=peer/peer identity=] to <dfn>known peer</dfn>: a [=peer=] the user agent has discovered or had a session with. Whether a known peer is [=/authenticated=] is not stored; it follows from the [=local trust store=].
: <dfn>peer grants</dfn>
:: An [=/ordered map=] from [=/origin=] to the [=/set=] of [=peer/peer identities=] that the origin may use.

These are kept consistent:

* Removing a [=trust anchor=] removes the [=peer grants=] of peers that are no longer [=/authenticated=].
* Removing a [=known peer=] removes it from all [=peer grants=].
* Clearing an origin's site data or permissions removes its [=peer grants=].

Note: [=Peer grants=] are origin-level while the [=local trust store=] is shared by all origins, so that the user doesn't need to authenticate the same peer again for every origin. Origins are kept apart by the [=peer grants=] and by the [=protocol/requesting origin=] of each session. [=Peer grants=] could be stored as the origin's permission data in the [=permission store=], as Web Bluetooth does for its allowed devices.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#24): Validate that a [=local trust store=] shared by all origins is appropriate, or whether [=trust anchors=] should be partitioned, for example per top-level site.

The user agent may persist its [=local trust store=], [=known peers=] and/or [=peer grants=]. If the user agent chooses to do so, it must do so in accordance with the persistent state rules of the [=transport binding=] in use. Such state is said to be <dfn>persisted</dfn>. Results of [=discover peers=], such as addresses, are not part of this state. The user agent should let the user view and remove [=trust anchors=], [=known peers=] and [=peer grants=].

Peer advertisement {#peer-advertisement}
----------------------------------------

The user agent can make itself discoverable by advertising, using [=start advertising=].

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to start advertising.

When advertising, the user agent must listen for incoming peer connections. In case an [=incoming session=] is received, the peer is added to the [=known peers map=]. The user agent must not directly provide access to the incoming peer connection to any origin. Instead, the user agent must prompt to [grant peer](#peer-grant) access to the origin that initiated peer advertisement.

The user agent must deliver an [=incoming session=] only to a started {{LP2PReceiver}} that <dfn lt="allows an origin">allows</dfn> the session's [=protocol/requesting origin=], and close the session if there is none. An {{LP2PReceiver}} [=allows an origin=] if that origin is [=same origin=] with the receiver's [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=], or with the [=url/origin=] of an entry of its {{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}}.

Peer discovery {#peer-discovery}
--------------------------------

When asked to <dfn>start local peer discovery</dfn>, the user agent runs [=discover peers=].

If a peer is discovered, the user agent should add it to the [=known peers map=]. The user agent must never expose the full result of peer discovery with an origin. Instead, the user agent must [=acquire a local peer grant=] to grant access to a peer for the origin that initiated the peer request.

Peer authentication {#peer-authentication}
------------------------------------------

A <dfn>trust anchor</dfn> is a key that the user agent trusts to authenticate [=peers=]. It has:

: <dfn for="trust anchor">key</dfn>
:: The key that peers' credentials chain to. What the credentials are, and how they chain to a key, is defined by the [=transport binding=].
: <dfn for="trust anchor">scope</dfn>
:: Either <dfn for="trust anchor/scope">one peer</dfn>, a single peer that the anchor vouches for, or <dfn for="trust anchor/scope">vouching</dfn>, any peer the anchor vouches for, within limits defined by the [=transport binding=].

Adding a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] [=trust anchor=] removes the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] [=trust anchors=] with the same [=trust anchor/key=], which it covers.

A [=trust anchor=] is added to the [=local trust store=] only by a <dfn>trust ceremony</dfn>: a run of [=authenticate a session=] in which the user takes part, for example by entering a PIN or scanning a QR code. A [=trust ceremony=] with a peer adds a [=trust anchor=] scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=]: that peer. A [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] [=trust anchor=] is only added by a [=trust ceremony=] with the vouching party itself.

A [=peer=] is said to be <dfn>authenticated</dfn> when its credentials chain to the [=trust anchor/key=] of a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], and the peer is within that anchor's [=trust anchor/scope=].

A peer's credentials may change over time without changing its [=peer/peer identity=], as long as they chain to the same [=trust anchor/key=]. Whether a [=trust anchor/key=] can be replaced with continuity is defined by the [=transport binding=]; if so, the new key replaces the old one in every [=trust anchor=] that has it. Otherwise, a new key needs a new [=trust ceremony=].

Issue: Define how a peer can move to another [=trust anchor=]'s [=trust anchor/key=] while keeping its [=peer/peer identity=] and [=peer grants=], for example by proving that it holds both keys.

When asked to <dfn>authenticate a local peer</dfn>, the user agent must [=establish local peer connection|establish a session=] with the peer, unless one exists, and run [=authenticate a session=] on it. The peer is [=/authenticated=] if this succeeds.

<div class="example" id="example-trust-anchors">
    * A device paired directly that uses its own key, which makes it <dfn>self-anchored</dfn>: its key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], that device.
    * A device paired directly whose credentials are issued by a hub: the hub's key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], that device. The device's credentials can rotate freely, and other devices of the hub are not trusted.
    * A hub the user chose to trust for its devices, through a [=trust ceremony=] with the hub: the hub's key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=]. It replaces the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] anchors for that hub's devices, which keep the same [=peer/peer identity=]. Removing it removes trust in all of the hub's devices.
</div>

Note: [=Trust anchors=] may also be used to validate HTTPS servers on the [=local communication medium=], each constrained to a namespace of local names; see the separate [Local HTTPS](https://wicg.github.io/local-peer-to-peer/local-https.html) draft.

Peer grant {#peer-grant}
------------------------------------

The user agent, with the user's consent, must [=acquire a local peer grant=] for an origin to get access to a peer.

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to grant an origin access to a peer. For enhanced privacy protection, the user agent must provide means to dismiss any related user interface and this action must not be detectable by script or have script-observable side-effects. The user interface may provide means to revoke the grant or to make the grant [=persisted=].

When asked to <dfn>acquire a local peer grant</dfn>, the user agent displays the [=known peers=] to the user through its native user interface. When the user selects a peer from the user interface, the user agent must check the selected peer is [=/authenticated=], and run the [=authenticate a local peer=] algorithm otherwise, and, if the user grants access, add the selected peer's [=peer/peer identity=] to the origin's [=peer grants=].

Transport abstraction {#transport-abstraction}
==============================================

The API and its algorithms are defined against the abstract concepts and operations in this section. A <dfn>transport binding</dfn> defines how a concrete protocol implements them. This specification defines one [=transport binding=], in [[#osp-binding]]. Which bindings a user agent implements is [=implementation-defined=]. A [=peer=] is associated with the binding through which it was discovered or through which its session arrived, and all operations involving that peer use that binding.

A [=transport binding=] must provide end-to-end confidentiality and integrity for all communication with a peer, including when the path to the peer goes through an intermediary.

Peers and sessions {#transport-concepts}
----------------------------------------

A [=peer=] has the following, provided by its [=transport binding=]. None of them is exposed to an origin.

: <dfn for="peer">peer identity</dfn>
:: An opaque value that identifies the peer, stable across sessions, scoped to the [=transport binding=]. It is derived from the [=trust anchor/key=] that the peer's credentials chain to and the peer's subject under it; for a [=self-anchored=] peer, the subject is its own key. It is only trusted once the peer is [=/authenticated=]. The [=known peers map=] and [=peer grants=] are keyed on it.
: <dfn for="peer">display name</dfn>
:: A name for the peer device, for display in user agent user interface, or null. It is asserted by the peer's user agent, not by script. Trusted only if obtained after the peer is [=/authenticated=], or by other means the user agent considers trustworthy; otherwise, such as during [=discover peers=], it is a display hint.
: <dfn for="peer">nickname</dfn>
:: The nickname given by the remote page when it started advertising, or null. It is chosen by script and never verified.

A <dfn for="protocol">Local Peer-to-Peer session</dfn> is a confidential connection with one [=peer=]. It is <dfn for="protocol">authenticated</dfn> if the credentials the peer presented in it chain to a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], which verifies the [=peer/peer identity=] of the peer for it.

A [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] that is exposed to an origin is not shared with other origins. A binding may carry several sessions with the same peer over one underlying connection, or use a separate connection for each.

For each [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], the side that established it with [=establish local peer connection=] is the <dfn for="protocol">client</dfn>, and the side that reported it as an [=incoming session=] is the <dfn for="protocol">server</dfn>. Once established, the session is symmetric: either side can use any operation on it, except that:

* the [=protocol/server=] validates the [=protocol/requesting origin=];
* where the binding needs it to avoid collisions, identifiers such as [=data channel/id=]s and streams follow the role;
* only the [=protocol/client=] opens any additional underlying connection for the session.

A [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] that is exposed to an origin has a <dfn for="protocol">requesting origin</dfn>: the [=/origin=] on whose behalf the [=protocol/client=] established it. The [=protocol/client=] conveys it inside the [=protocol/authenticated=] session, bound to that session (for example by a per-origin credential or the exporter context; defined by the [=transport binding=]). The [=protocol/server=] checks it as described in [[#peer-advertisement]].

The [=protocol/requesting origin=] is asserted by the peer's user agent. The binding guarantees that it is consistent for the session, not that it is authentic: it does not prove that the remote page was served by that origin. User agents must not grant anything that belongs to the [=protocol/requesting origin=], such as its permissions, storage or credentials, on the strength of it. This is the same model as the `Origin` header in [[FETCH]].

Device keys, credentials and anything else that would let an origin authenticate as the user agent must not be exposed to origins. User agents should instead expose per-session keying material exporters, like those of [WebTransport over HTTP/3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-webtrans-http3/), so that applications can prove their origins with credentials their own origin issued.

Issue: Define the keying material exporter API, for example an `exportKeyingMaterial()` method as considered for WebTransport, and whether its context includes the [=protocol/requesting origin=].

A <dfn>data channel</dfn> is a reliable, ordered, message-preserving, bidirectional channel on a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]. Either side can open one. It has an <dfn for="data channel">id</dfn>, an integer between 0 and 65,534 that is unique within the session, a <dfn for="data channel">label</dfn> and a <dfn for="data channel">protocol</dfn>. A message on a [=data channel=] is a sequence of bytes, marked as either text or binary.

Operations {#transport-operations}
----------------------------------

A [=transport binding=] provides the following operations. Any of them can fail, including because the peer does not support it.

: To <dfn>start advertising</dfn> given a nickname
:: Make the user agent discoverable through [=discover peers=] on other user agents, with the given [=peer/nickname=] if the binding can carry it, and accept sessions from peers. This returns an <dfn>advertisement</dfn>. Each [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] a peer establishes with it is reported as an <dfn>incoming session</dfn> for that [=advertisement=]. To <dfn>stop advertising</dfn> given an [=advertisement=] ends both.
: To <dfn>discover peers</dfn>
:: Report the [=peers=] advertising on the [=local communication medium=], as they appear and disappear, until asked to <dfn>stop discovering peers</dfn>. A discovered [=peer/peer identity=] is not verified.
: To <dfn>establish local peer connection</dfn> with a [=peer=]
:: Establish a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] with the peer. On the peer, the session is reported as an [=incoming session=].
: To <dfn>authenticate a session</dfn>
:: Mutually authenticate the two sides of a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] and verify the [=peer/peer identity=] of the peer, so that the session is [=protocol/authenticated=]. If the peer's credentials do not chain to a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], this is a [=trust ceremony=], which requires user interaction on one or both devices and adds a [=trust anchor=] scoped to that peer. Either side can initiate it.
: To <dfn>open a data channel</dfn> on an [=protocol/authenticated=] [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] given a label, a protocol and an optional id
:: Open a [=data channel=] with that [=data channel/label=] and [=data channel/protocol=], and with the given [=data channel/id=] or, if none is given, one chosen by the binding. The other side is notified of an <dfn>incoming data channel</dfn>, which its user agent accepts or refuses. The operation succeeds if it is accepted.
: To <dfn>send data on a channel</dfn> given a [=data channel=] and a message
:: Queue the message for delivery on the [=data channel=]. The other side is notified of a <dfn>received message</dfn>.
: To <dfn>close a data channel</dfn>
:: Close the [=data channel=] in both directions. The other side is notified that it was closed.
: To <dfn>close a session</dfn>
:: Close the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], all of its [=data channels=] and any WebTransport session opened over it. The other side is notified that it was closed. The binding also reports a session as closed when the underlying transport fails.
: To <dfn>open a WebTransport session</dfn> over an [=protocol/authenticated=] [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]
:: Establish a session with the same peer that provides the capabilities of a WebTransport session as defined in [[!webtransport]], bound to the same [=peer/peer identity=]. The other side is notified of an incoming WebTransport session.

Note: [=Data channels=] ({{LP2PDataChannel}}) and WebTransport sessions ({{LP2PQuicTransport}}) are two independent ways to communicate over a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]; neither is built on the other.

Issue(29): Decide whether to keep both data channels and WebTransport sessions, or converge on one.

<section algorithm="LP2PReceiver">

LP2PReceiver Interface {#lp2p-receiver}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PReceiver</dfn> interface allows advertising on the [=local communication medium=], enabling other peers to discover and connect.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PReceiver : EventTarget {
  constructor(optional LP2PReceiverOptions options = {});
  attribute EventHandler onconnection;
  Promise&lt;undefined&gt; start();
};
</pre>

LP2PReceiverOptions {#lp2p-receiver-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PReceiverOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
    sequence&lt;USVString&gt; allowedOrigins = [];
};
</pre>

: <dfn dict-member for="LP2PReceiverOptions">allowedOrigins</dfn>
:: A list of URLs whose [=url/origins=], in addition to the receiver's own origin, the receiver [=allows an origin|allows=] as the [=protocol/requesting origin=] of an [=incoming session=]. By default, only same-origin sessions are delivered.

Issue: Decide the granularity of {{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}}, the [=protocol/requesting origin=] and [=peer grants=]: per origin or per site, and whether to allow wildcards or any origin.

LP2PConnectionEvent {#lp2p-receiver-connection-event}
----------------------------------------------------

Issue: In general, when defining a new interface that inherits from Event please always ask feedback from the WHATWG or the W3C WebApps WG community. See [defining event interfaces](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#defining-event-interfaces).

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnectionEvent : Event {
    constructor(DOMString type, LP2PConnectionEventInit connectionEventInitDict);
    readonly attribute LP2PConnection connection;
};

dictionary LP2PConnectionEventInit : EventInit {
    required LP2PConnection connection;
};
</pre>

Events {#lp2p-receiver-events}
------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PReceiver}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PReceiver"><code>connection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnectionEvent}}</td>
      <td>An incoming connection is received.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-receiver-event-handlers}
----------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the [[#lp2p-receiver|LP2PReceiver]] interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver"><code>onconnection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PReceiver/connection}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Examples {#lp2p-receiver-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a receiver to listen for connections:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});
receiver.onconnection = e => {
    console.log("Connection established!");
    const conn = e.connection;
};

// Blocks until permission is received.
await receiver.start();
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PRequest">

The LP2PRequest Interface {#lp2p-request}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PRequest</dfn> interface represents a request for a connection to another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PRequest {
    constructor(optional LP2PRequestOptions options = {});
    Promise&lt;LP2PConnection&gt; start();
};
</pre>

LP2PRequestOptions {#lp2p-request-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PRequestOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
};
</pre>

Examples {#lp2p-request-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a request for a connection:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

// Blocks until connection is received.
const conn = await request.start();
console.log("Connection established!");
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PConnection">

The LP2PConnection Interface {#lp2p-connection}
===============================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PConnection</dfn> interface represents a connection with another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnection : EventTarget {
};
</pre>

LP2PQuicTransport Interface Extensions {#lp2p-connection-quic-transport-extensions}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

This {{LP2PQuicTransport}} extension allows opening a QuicTransport using an existing connection. In this case, the already established permission and grants should be used.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
partial interface LP2PQuicTransport {
  constructor(LP2PConnection connection,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportInit quicTransportDict = {});
};
</pre>

Examples {#lp2p-connection-examples}
----------------------------------------

Example: Open a {{LP2PQuicTransport}} using an existing connection as receiver:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});
receiver.onconnection = async e => {
    const conn = e.connection;
    
    const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(conn);

    // Blocks until transport is ready.
    await transport.ready;
};

// Blocks until permission is received.
await receiver.start();
</pre>

Example: Open a {{LP2PQuicTransport}} using an existing connection as requester:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

// Blocks until connection is received.
const conn = await request.start();

const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(conn);

// Blocks until transport is ready.
await transport.ready;
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PDataChannel">

The LP2PDataChannel Interface {#lp2p-data-channel}
==========================================

The LP2PDataChannel interface represents a bi-directional data channel between two peers. An LP2PDataChannel is created via a [factory method](#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions) on a LP2PConnection object. It is backed by a [=data channel=] on the connection's [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=].

Note: The LP2PDataChannel interface is purposefully kept as close as possible to the [RTCDataChannel](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/#rtcdatachannel) interface defined in [[!webrtc]]. The aim is to allow seamless transition of developers that are familiar with WebRTC as well as allowing libraries to easily work with both the [[!webrtc]] and LP2P API.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PDataChannel : EventTarget {
  constructor(USVString label,
              optional LP2PDataChannelInit dataChannelDict = {});
  readonly attribute USVString label;
  readonly attribute USVString protocol;
  readonly attribute unsigned short? id;
  attribute EventHandler onopen;
  attribute EventHandler onerror;
  attribute EventHandler onclosing;
  attribute EventHandler onclose;
  undefined close();
  attribute EventHandler onmessage;
  attribute BinaryType binaryType;
  undefined send((USVString or Blob or ArrayBuffer or ArrayBufferView) data);
};
</pre>

LP2PDataChannelInit {#lp2p-data-channel-init}
----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PDataChannelInit {
  USVString protocol = "";
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short id;
};
</pre>

